  data:  # 63dd
    kind: invariant  # 63dd
    title: "Auto-sync cleanup race: post-snapshot tracker mutations silently destroyed by sync.py cleanup"  # 63dd
    status: violated  # 63dd
    priority: 2  # 63dd
    parent:  # 63dd
    tags:  # 63dd
    - tracker_infrastructure  # 63dd
    - data_loss  # 63dd
    isbefore: []  # 63dd
    duplicate_of: []  # 63dd
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 63dd
    pr_ref:  # 63dd
    description: "## Empirical observation\n\nDuring the 2026-05-26 session, three tracker mutations were lost:\n1. **WI-dilab (entire item)** — ops file deleted by auto-sync cleanup\n2. **WI-hupaz `done` update** — ops file reset to HEAD by auto-sync cleanup\n3. **INV-hujog `pending_validation`** — survived only because it was written AFTER the cleanup ran\n\nDiscovered during a notes-vs-tracker consistency check in the following session. The transcript confirms all three `tracker update` / `tracker add` commands succeeded at the CLI level. The ops were written to disk but then destroyed before they could be committed.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`sync.py` lines 1486-1527: after a successful sync+merge, the cleanup:\n1. `git checkout HEAD -- <ops paths>` — resets modified tracked ops to match HEAD (line 1490)\n2. `git ls-files --others` + `unlink` — deletes untracked ops files (lines 1503-1518)\n3. `git merge --ff-only origin/dev` — fast-forwards local dev to include the sync commit (line 1519)\n\nThe intent is that the sync commit already contains these files, so they'll be restored by the ff-only merge. But there's a race window: tracker mutations that occur between the `git add` (step 3 of sync, line 1146) and the cleanup (post-merge) are not in the sync commit. The cleanup deletes/resets them anyway.\n\nFor **untracked files** (new items created mid-session): the `git add -- .agent/tracker*/.ops/` captures them at snapshot time. If a new ops file is created between snapshot and cleanup, it gets `unlink`'d but was never in the sync commit. The ff-only merge doesn't restore it.\n\nFor **modified tracked files**: `git checkout HEAD --` resets to the pre-sync HEAD. Even if the sync commit contains newer ops, the reset happens BEFORE the ff-only merge, so post-snapshot mutations are wiped. The ff-only merge restores the file to the sync-commit state (which lacks the post-snapshot mutations).\n\n## Reproduction conditions\n\nThe race is most likely when:\n- Pending line count is near-threshold (~70-79) when a large batch of work completes\n- The agent performs tracker mutations WHILE auto-sync is running (push + CI poll + merge can take 1-5 minutes)\n- The agent is on the base branch (dev) when auto-sync fires (required for the cleanup path at line 1486)\n\n## Impact\n\n- New tracker items created mid-session can be silently destroyed (entire item lost, no error)\n- Status transitions on existing items can be silently reverted (done → in_progress, pending_validation → violated)\n- Discussion entries added mid-session can be silently lost\n- The agent receives success from `tracker update/add` but the ops never persist\n\n## Proposed fix directions\n\n**Option A (narrowest): post-ff-only diff guard.** After the ff-only merge at line 1519, re-check whether any ops files in the working tree differ from the now-current HEAD. If they do, those are post-snapshot mutations that survived in memory but not in the sync commit. Skip the `unlink`/`checkout` for those files (or better: move them to a recovery spool and replay after merge).\n\n**Option B (structural): snapshot-and-hold.** Before the `git add` at line 1146, snapshot the set of ops files and their mtimes. In the cleanup phase, only reset/unlink files whose mtime matches the snapshot. Any file with a newer mtime has post-snapshot mutations that must be preserved.\n\n**Option C (belt-and-suspenders): session-end forced sync.** Add a `_force_sync_if_pending()` call to the session-end hook. This doesn't fix the race, but it ensures that ops written after the last auto-sync aren't lost when the session ends. Orthogonal to A/B.\n\n## Related\n\n- AGENTS.md documents the `auto-pr` clobber hazard (\"do not perform tracker mutations while an auto-pr run is in flight\") but this is a different mechanism — the tracker's own auto-sync cleanup\n- `sync.py:1480-1485` comment about WI-lufal documents the design intent for the `merge_succeeded` guard\n- The safeguard in `auto-pr` (index-only ops exclusion, lines 1233-1265) is separate and worked correctly in this incident"  # 63dd
    fields:  # 63dd
      statement: "Tracker mutations performed between auto-sync's git-add snapshot and its post-merge cleanup must not be destroyed by the cleanup. No silent data loss."  # 63dd
      root_cause: "sync.py cleanup (lines 1486-1527) unconditionally resets tracked ops to HEAD and deletes untracked ops after a successful sync+merge, without checking whether those files received post-snapshot mutations during the sync's push+CI+merge window."  # 63dd
